    {
      "id": "E14",
      "chapter": 14,
      "slug": "14-arrays-and-stability",
      "section": "14.3",
      "title": "Dispersed variance can still leave jumps",
      "lang": "en",
      "html": "<figure class=\"batch2\" id=\"visual-E14\" data-batch2=\"E14\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>E14</span><h3>Dispersed variance can still leave jumps</h3></header><p class=\"\">Both rows have total variance 1 and variance 1/n per term. One keeps its nonzero atoms at ±1; the other brings them toward 0.</p><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-array-n=\"4\" aria-pressed=\"false\">n=4</button><button type=\"button\" data-array-n=\"16\" aria-pressed=\"true\">n=16</button><button type=\"button\" data-array-n=\"64\" aria-pressed=\"false\">n=64</button></div><div class=\"b2-pair\"><section class=\"b2-panel\"><h4>Rare, fixed-size jumps</h4><img class=\"ex-svg\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v2/E14-en-16-rare.svg\" alt=\"Rare atoms stay at minus and plus one\" data-rare-scene><p class=\"b2-result\" >Second moment outside threshold: 1</p></section><section class=\"b2-panel\"><h4>Every increment gets small</h4><img class=\"ex-svg\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v2/E14-en-16-small.svg\" alt=\"Fair small increments move toward zero\" data-small-scene><p class=\"b2-result\" >Second moment outside threshold: 0</p></section></div><p class=\"b2-note\">Pale band: |x|≤1/2. Change n to separate atom positions from probability areas.</p><figcaption id=\"visual-E14-end\">Terms are independent within each row. Left: P(Xnk=±1)=1/(2n), P(0)=1−1/n. Right: P(Ynk=±1/√n)=1/2. Both are centered with total variance 1 and maximal variance 1/n→0. At fixed ε=1/2, their Lindeberg quantities are 1 and 0 for all displayed n≥4 (strict >). On the left, the probability of at least one nonzero term tends to 1−e⁻1; cancellation means this is not the probability of a nonzero sum. Its sum tends to compound Poisson with rate 1 and fair ±1 marks; the right sum tends to a standard Gaussian. Disk areas are probabilities and horizontal positions are values. Boxes label terms 1, 2 and n, with an ellipsis for the omitted middle terms; each row has exactly n terms. Shading marks a threshold, not density.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v2/E14.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/E14.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "Terms are independent within each row. Left: P(Xnk=±1)=1/(2n), P(0)=1−1/n. Right: P(Ynk=±1/√n)=1/2. Both are centered with total variance 1 and maximal variance 1/n→0. At fixed ε=1/2, their Lindeberg quantities are 1 and 0 for all displayed n≥4 (strict >). On the left, the probability of at least one nonzero term tends to 1−e⁻1; cancellation means this is not the probability of a nonzero sum. Its sum tends to compound Poisson with rate 1 and fair ±1 marks; the right sum tends to a standard Gaussian. Disk areas are probabilities and horizontal positions are values. Boxes label terms 1, 2 and n, with an ellipsis for the omitted middle terms; each row has exactly n terms. Shading marks a threshold, not density.",
      "status": "adopted working visual; local clarification pending author check",
